    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, the kingdom took its name from León, the royal seat from 914, and ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> plain.</li><li>Its kings claimed Visigothic heritage and an imperial title, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 1085.</li><li>The Cortes of León of 1188 included townsmen; in 1230 León was united permanently with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Pantheon of the Kings at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-san-isidoro">San Isidoro</a> in León, burial place of Leonese royalty, preserves twelfth-century Romanesque wall paintings that include a calendar of the labours of the months.</p></aside>
    </section>

