    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias.jpg" alt="Alfonso III of Asturias" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias">Alfonso III of Asturias</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Testamento.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pelagius, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of León grew out of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, the small Christian realm founded in the mountains of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> after the Muslim conquest of 711. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias">Alfonso III</a> (r. 866–910) the Asturian kings pushed south to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a>, resettling the thinly populated plain with colonists from the north and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and fortifying towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a>, Toro and the old Roman camp of León. In 910 Alfonso III was deposed by his sons, and his realm was divided: García I took the lands south of the mountains, centred on León, Ordoño received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fruela-ii-of-asturias">Fruela</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturia">Asturias</a>. When García died in 914 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordono-ii-of-leon">Ordoño II</a> reunited most of the inheritance and made León his royal seat, and historians date the kingdom's name from this transfer of the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>. The new kingdom thus combined the mountain core of Asturias, the populous region of Galicia in the north-west and the frontier lands of the Douro basin, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in the east, governed by counts, as its most exposed and increasingly independent district. The Leonese kings presented themselves as heirs of the Visigothic monarchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, a claim that gave ideological purpose to their wars against al-Andalus and to the resettlement (<em>repoblación</em>) of the lands to the south.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/salamanca"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salamanca.jpg" alt="Salamanca" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:68436-Salamanca_(49093713952)_edited.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">xiquinhosilva from Cacau, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>León was an agrarian frontier society. Its wealth lay in grain, vines and above all livestock, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and sheep were easier to save from raids than crops. Free peasant settlers received land and privileges under charters (<em>fueros</em>) in return for settling the frontier, alongside monasteries and nobles who claimed land by <em>presura</em>, the occupation of vacant land. Society comprised nobles, clergy, free peasants and dependent cultivators, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozarabs">Mozarabs</a>, Arabic-speaking Christians who migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> bringing their own artistic traditions, and Jewish communities in the towns. The city of León, seat of the court and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astorga">Astorga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> were the main centres. Trade was modest until the eleventh century, when tribute in gold (<em>parias</em>) from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kingdoms of al-Andalus enriched the court and the Church, and the pilgrimage road to the shrine of Saint James at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> brought French pilgrims, merchants and settlers, with communities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> growing up at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahagun">Sahagún</a> and León. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cluniac-reforms">Cluniac</a> monks, generously endowed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a>, reformed monastic life, and the Roman liturgy replaced the old Hispanic, or Mozarabic, rite in the 1080s. A royal coinage was first minted under Alfonso VI, a sign of the economy's growth in the eleventh century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-valdejunquera"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-valdejunquera.jpg" alt="Battle of Valdejunquera" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-valdejunquera">Battle of Valdejunquera</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Iberian_Peninsula_910-es.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Crates, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Leonese warfare combined royal campaigns with constant frontier raiding by nobles, town militias and settlers who were obliged to serve on horseback or on foot under their <em>fueros</em>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordono-ii-of-leon">Ordoño II</a> won a victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-esteban-de-gormaz">San Esteban de Gormaz</a> in 917 but was badly defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-valdejunquera">Valdejunquera</a> in 920. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-ii-of-leon">Ramiro II</a> (r. 931–951), allied with the count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernan-gonzalez-of-castile">Fernán González</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-simancas">Battle of Simancas</a> in 939, one of the few great Christian victories of the period, and resettled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tormes">Tormes</a> valley around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a>. In the late tenth century the balance turned: al-Mansur, the chief minister of the caliphate known in Spanish as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a>, led dozens of campaigns into the north, sacking the city of León in the late 980s and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> in 997. After the collapse of the caliphate the advantage returned to the Christians. Ferdinand I took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> in 1064, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, the old Visigothic capital, in 1085, but the Almoravid victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sagrajas">Battle of Sagrajas</a> in 1086 and the death of his heir at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ucles">Uclés</a> in 1108 checked the advance. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX</a> the frontier moved south again, helped by the military orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-alcantara">Alcántara</a>, until Alfonso IX took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caceres">Cáceres</a> in 1229 and Mérida and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a> in 1230.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/visigothic-code"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/visigothic-code.jpg" alt="Visigothic Code" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/visigothic-code">Visigothic Code</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LiberIudiciorum01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The king of León ruled with a royal court (<em>curia regia</em>) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> and palace officials, and through counts and royal agents (<em>merinos</em>) who governed districts, collected dues and administered justice. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigothic-code">Liber Iudiciorum</a></em>, the Visigothic law code, remained the basis of law, supplemented by local charters and custom. The kings stressed their descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, and from the tenth century some styled themselves emperor (<em>imperator</em>), a claim to precedence over the other Christian rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> called himself emperor of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII</a> was crowned emperor in León in 1135. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-v-of-leon">Alfonso V</a>'s <em>fuero</em> of León, issued at a royal council in 1017 or 1020, set out rules for the city and the kingdom and became a model for later charters. The most celebrated institution was the assembly summoned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX</a> in León in 1188, at the start of his reign, where representatives of the towns sat alongside bishops and nobles; the king promised to respect the laws, to seek their counsel on war and peace and to protect property. Its decrees, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO</a> recognised in 2013 as the oldest documentary record of the European parliamentary system, have made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-leon-of-1188">Cortes of León of 1188</a> a landmark in the history of representative government. Alfonso IX also founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-salamanca">University of Salamanca</a> around 1218.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-san-isidoro"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basilica-of-san-isidoro.jpg" alt="Basilica of San Isidoro" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-san-isidoro">Basilica of San Isidoro</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bas%C3%ADlica_de_San_Isidoro_de_Le%C3%B3n._Fachada_principal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José Luis Filpo Cabana, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>León carried the Asturian idea of a Christian restoration of Visigothic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> into the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and its kings were the leading Christian rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> for much of the tenth and eleventh centuries. Its expansion to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a> laid foundations for the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and the County of Portugal, granted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> to his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-count-of-portugal">Henry of Burgundy</a>, became the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso Henriques</a>, recognised by León in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zamora">Treaty of Zamora</a> in 1143. Culturally the kingdom produced some of the finest art of early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The illuminated Beatus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, copies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beatus-of-liebana">Beatus of Liébana</a>'s commentary on the Apocalypse, were painted in Leonese scriptoria in the Mozarabic style, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beatus-of-facundus">Beatus of Facundus</a> made for Ferdinand I and Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancha-of-leon">Sancha</a> in 1047. The church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-san-isidoro">San Isidoro</a> in León, which received the relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville">Isidore of Seville</a> in 1063, houses the Pantheon of the Kings, whose twelfth-century Romanesque paintings are among the best preserved in Europe. The pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>, promoted by the Leonese kings and by Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-gelmirez">Diego Gelmírez</a>, became one of the great routes of Christendom. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of 1188 is often cited as the earliest European parliament to include townsmen, and the Leonese language survives in the Asturian and Leonese dialects of today.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tamaron"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-tamaron.jpg" alt="Battle of Tamarón" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tamaron">Battle of Tamarón</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferdinand_I_of_Le%C3%B3n_cely.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>León's history was bound up with that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, its eastern county turned rival. Castile under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernan-gonzalez-of-castile">Fernán González</a> became effectively autonomous in the mid-tenth century, and in 1037 Ferdinand I, count of Castile and son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III of Navarre</a>, killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bermudo-iii-of-leon">Bermudo III</a>, the last king of the old Leonese line, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tamaron">Battle of Tamarón</a> and took the Leonese throne by right of his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancha-of-leon">Sancha</a>. Ferdinand divided his kingdoms among his sons in 1065, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> reunited León, Castile and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> by 1072, and they remained together under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urraca-of-leon-and-castile">Urraca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII</a>. At Alfonso VII's death in 1157 León and Castile were separated again, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II</a> taking León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-castile">Sancho III</a> Castile. For the next seventy years the two kingdoms were rivals, competing for frontier territory and sometimes allying with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> against each other; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX</a> took no part in the Castilian victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Las Navas de Tolosa</a> in 1212. Alfonso IX's marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengaria-of-castile">Berenguela of Castile</a> produced a son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III</a>, who became king of Castile in 1217. When Alfonso IX died in 1230 he left León to his daughters by an earlier marriage, Sancha and Dulce, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-benavente">Treaty of Benavente</a>, negotiated by their mothers, gave the kingdom to Ferdinand III, uniting León and Castile permanently in the Crown of Castile.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>León was the leading Christian kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in the tenth and eleventh centuries, carrying the Visigothic legacy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> south to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a>, producing the Beatus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and the Pantheon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-san-isidoro">San Isidoro</a>, and summoning in 1188 an assembly with town representatives often called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s first parliament.</em></p>

